The most important distinction for you
As a Senior/AGM P&M candidate, don't present yourself as the person who merely operates these machines.

Your responsibility is:

PLAN → SELECT → DEPLOY → CALCULATE → INSPECT → MAINTAIN → CONTROL → OPTIMIZE → REPORT.

For example, if management says:
“We have a 120-ton precast girder to erect over a 35-m span. How will you plan the operation?”

Your answer should naturally move through:

Girder weight → C.G. → lifting points → crane/launcher selection → radius → load chart → ground bearing → lifting accessories → tandem/single lift → transport → temporary support → bearing → erection sequence → wind limit → communication → trial lift → final lift → documentation.

That is P&M Head-level bridge knowledge.

One important point: crane capacities, lifting accessories, launching-girder configuration, prestressing force, jack capacity and barge stability are engineered values—not generic rules of thumb. The approved lifting/erection method statement, manufacturer's load charts, structural calculations and project specifications must govern the actual operation. NHAI's technical schedule also requires erection plant/equipment capacity and suitability to be acceptable to the Engineer. NHAI

